{"tool": "finish", "summary": "Added the per-user `max_feeds` limit (0 = unlimited). The build and existing unit and integration tests pass; nothing exercises the new behaviour yet, so it is untested beyond compiling.\n\n- **Schema and model**: a new migration adds `users.max_feeds` (int, default 0). The field is returned as `max_feeds` on the user object and accepted in the user modification request.\n- **Who can change it**: on `PUT /v1/users/{userID}`, a non-admin request containing `max_feeds` gets 403, even if the value is unchanged. A negative value gets 400. The column is written only by a new `SetUserMaxFeeds` storage method called from that admin path; `UpdateUser` does not touch it, so settings forms and other update paths cannot alter it.\n- **Enforcement**: the limit is checked in `storage.CreateFeed`, in the same transaction as the insert, with a lock on the user row. Every feed-creation path goes through that function (API, OPML import, web UI, Google Reader API), so concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit either.\n- **`POST /v1/feeds`**: returns 400 and creates nothing when the user is at the limit. It checks before fetching the feed, so no outbound request is made.\n- **`POST /v1/import`**: imports feeds until the limit is reached, then stops and returns 400. Feeds imported before that point stay, so the user never exceeds `max_feeds`, but an oversized import is partial rather than rejected outright.\n\nThings you might not expect:\n- The web UI and Google Reader paths are blocked by the storage check but report it as a generic database error; I did not add a translated message, to avoid touching every locale file.\n- There is no admin UI field for `max_feeds`; it can only be set through the API.\n- Lowering `max_feeds` below a user's current feed count deletes nothing; it only blocks new feeds.\n- I updated the `@effects` tags on `storage.CreateFeed`, the OPML `Import` and the API import handler, but not on the web UI callers of `Import`, which now also read `users`.", "t": 1794463786.469}
